// DRed PHASES 2 AND 3 AS AN EXECUTABLE SPECIFICATION — HARVESTED FROM THE
// INTERPRETER BEFORE IT IS DELETED (P5).
//
// ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND WHY IT CANNOT BE WRITTEN LATER ────────────────
// `stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos :: fn dred` is TODAY the only executable
// account of the compact→re-derive composition. The static tier emits phase 1
// only (`__wql_..._od` / `__wql_..._odp`, pinned by
// tests/logos/pass/wql_incr_rel_od_phase1.logos); it emits no compact and no
// re-derive. At P5 `dred` is deleted and there is nothing left to ask.
//
// ── WHAT AN ANSWER ORACLE CANNOT SEE, MEASURED ────────────────────────────
// Retraction is three phases: (1) OVER-DELETE every fact with ANY derivation
// through a retracted edge, (2) COMPACT the total to S = T ∖ OD, (3) RE-DERIVE,
// which puts back RD = the over-deleted facts that still have an all-survivor
// derivation. The FINAL answer is closure(PRES) whether phase 1 over-deletes a
// little or a lot — phase 3 repairs the difference. So a fixture that asserts
// only `snapshot()` is blind to every defect that moves work between the
// phases, and those are exactly the defects that kill INCREMENTALITY: an
// over-broad phase 1 degenerates to "delete everything, recompute", which is
// still correct and no longer incremental. THIS FILE ASSERTS THE INTERMEDIATE
// SETS, which is the whole point of it.
//
// The three intermediates were function-local in `dred` (`od_rows`, and the
// post-compact `self.ptot`) and the epoch's single kind-3 TraceStep records only
// the NET delta across all three phases. Three read-only harvest latches were
// added for this file and are labelled as crutches at their definition:
//     IncrRec::od_len / od_ab            — phase 1's OD, as a set
//     IncrRec::dred_compact_len          — |S|, what phase 2 left
//     IncrRec::dred_rederive_len         — |RD|, what phase 3 put back
//
// ── THE THREE WITNESSES (the corpus pattern), AND WHO TAKES WHICH SLOT ─────
//  (1) HAND-DERIVED CONSTANTS — every cell lists T, OD, S, RD and the final
//      answer literally, computed by hand in the comment above it.
//  (2) AN INDEPENDENT ORACLE OF A DIFFERENT SHAPE — `automaton_od` below: the
//      product of the graph with the two-state automaton "have I used a deleted
//      edge yet", OD = {(x,z) : (z,1) reachable from (x,0)}. It contains no
//      delta, no fixpoint over a rel, no seed/propagate split, and — the point
//      — it is NOT the last-deleted-edge DECOMPOSITION that the phase-1 fixture
//      uses as its oracle, so a wrong decomposition theorem cannot be shared
//      between the two files. `naive_closure` (P := P ∪ P∘E to quiescence) is
//      the second half of the oracle, for T, for closure(PRES) and for S.
//  (3) THE ENGINE — here the INTERPRETER (`Query::incremental_rec` + `epoch`).
//      ⚠ THE STATIC TIER TAKES SLOT (3) LATER, unchanged: when the emitter grows
//      compact and re-derive, add an arm that drives the emitted fns and asserts
//      against the SAME `automaton_od` / `naive_closure` / hand constants. Do
//      NOT add the interpreter as an oracle for it — `dred` and the emitter run
//      THE SAME ALGORITHM, and an oracle that shares the algorithm is not an
//      oracle.
//
// ── THE CLOSED FORMS, DERIVED FROM THE SEMANTICS ──────────────────────────
//   T   = closure(ALL)
//   OD  = { (x,z) : some walk x→z in ALL uses an edge of DE }        (witness 2)
//   S   = T ∖ OD  = { f ∈ T : EVERY derivation of f is all-survivor }
//   RD  = closure(PRES) ∖ S  = OD ∩ closure(PRES)
//   final = S ⊎ RD = closure(PRES)
// RD > 0 is what makes phase 3 load-bearing; two cells here require it BY NAME.
//
// ── COST OF A CATCH ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every assertion sits after `epoch()` has run all three phases to quiescence
// over the whole graph. Nothing is caught on a first push.
//
// ── CONTROLS RUN, ALL THREE MEASURED 2026-08-07 ───────────────────────────
// Each is the DELETION OF A WHOLE BLOCK of `fn dred` (never a flipped condition
// inside a shared branch), with `build/lib/logos/liblogos-*.a` dropped and the
// stdlib rebuilt, so what the artifact differs by is a named statement that is
// gone. Alongside each fixture run, a probe printed the engine's own
// (final, |OD|, |S|, |RD|) for the chain and the diamond.
//   BASELINE:   chain final=2 od=4 S=2 RD=0 · diamond final=4 od=2 S=3 RD=1
//
//  • DELETE the phase-1a `rctx_set_delta(sctxp, 0, 0, ptot0)` existing-rel⋈DE
//    seed term, so phase 1 seeds from the base bodies only.
//    → **exit 80** — CELL 1 (chain), check 20: |OD| came out 2, not 4.
//    ⚠ AND THE MEASUREMENT THIS WHOLE FILE EXISTS FOR: on the DIAMOND the same
//    broken engine returned final=4 — THE CORRECT ANSWER, BYTE FOR BYTE — while
//    its phase split moved od 2→1, S 3→4, RD 1→0. A fixture asserting only
//    `snapshot()` is GREEN on that graph. |OD| and |RD| are the only cells here
//    that speak, and they are exactly what P5 would have deleted unrecorded.
//  • DELETE the phase-3 re-derive fixpoint loop.
//    → **exit 170** — CELL 2 (diamond), check 40: |RD| = 0, want 1. The probe
//    shows the answer degraded too (diamond final 4→3), so this defect a plain
//    answer oracle would also catch — it is here to pin WHICH phase is missing.
//    Chain and cycle: chain stays green (its RD is 0 by construction), which is
//    why the diamond is not optional.
//  • DELETE the phase-2 compact filter (`if !dred_in_od(…)` → keep every row).
//    → **exit 90** — CELL 1 (chain), check 30: |S| = 6 = |T|, want 2. Probe:
//    chain final 2→6, diamond 4→5.
//
// ── REGIONS THIS FILE COULD NOT REACH, RECORDED WHERE THEY WERE LOOKED FOR ─
// Region 2 (the RUNTIME read/write split — `RelCtx.wr_rowsp`/`wr_hmp`, chosen by
//   `let rows_i = if whm != 0 { wr } else { rowsp[ri] }` in
//   stdlib/mem/deem/exec.logos, set by `rctx_set_wr` at incr_rec.logos's phase
//   1a): NOT DIRECTLY OBSERVABLE. `RelCtx`'s fields are package-private and no
//   accessor exposes which storage a body wrote. What IS pinned here is its
//   OBSERVABLE EFFECT: the OD set that phase 1a produced through the redirect,
//   asserted as an exact set against `automaton_od`. The static tier bought the
//   same effect by EMITTING a sibling instead of branching at runtime; the
//   equivalence of the two mechanisms is exactly "both produce this set", and
//   that is now written down on both sides of the fence against the same oracle.
// Region 3 (N-member SCCs generically at runtime, N > 8): UNREACHABLE THROUGH
//   THIS ENGINE, and the last cell of `main` asserts why — `incremental_rec`
//   refuses `reg.n != 1` outright ("multiple rels (mutual-recursion SCC) — out
//   of slice-5"). `dred` therefore never runs for ANY mutual-recursion SCC, at
//   any N, so the interpreter is NOT a witness for generic-N retraction and the
//   emitter's `if nm < 8i64` truncation has no runtime counterpart to compare
//   with. (The 8-cap itself is refused with a diagnostic in BOTH tiers —
//   `Query::compile: too many rel blocks (max 8)` in stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos
//   and `wql!: too many rel blocks (max 8)` in stdlib/mem/wql/plan_walker.logos
//   — so the emitter truncation is dead code behind a registry cap, NOT a silent
//   loss.)
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

pub schema Edge : code(0x0E07_0000_0000_0021) {
    src: i64 = 0,
    dst: i64 = 1,
}
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ Edge };

struct E { pub a: i64, pub b: i64 }
fn ed(a: i64, b: i64) -> E { return E { a: a, b: b }; }

// ── set utilities over Vec<(i64,i64)> ─────────────────────────────────────
fn pair_uniq(p: &mut Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    p.push((a, b));
    return true;
}

fn pair_has(p: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

// Mutual containment PLUS equal length, so a duplicate on either side is a
// disagreement rather than something containment absorbs.
fn same_set(x: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, y: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> bool {
    if x.len() != y.len() { return false; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = x.get(i);
        if !pair_has(y, c.0, c.1) { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < y.len() {
        let c2: (i64, i64) = y.get(j);
        if !pair_has(x, c2.0, c2.1) { return false; }
        j = j + 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}

// ── ORACLE HALF 1: from-scratch naive closure. `P := E ∪ (P ∘ E)` to
// quiescence, dedup by linear `==`. No delta, no semi-naive, no shadow set.
fn naive_closure(es: &Vec<E>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut p: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < es.len() { let e: E = es.get(i); pair_uniq(&mut p, e.a, e.b); i = i + 1i64; }
    let mut grew: bool = true;
    while grew {
        grew = false;
        let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
        while k < p.len() {
            let pr: (i64, i64) = p.get(k);
            let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
            while j < es.len() {
                let e2: E = es.get(j);
                if pr.1 == e2.a {
                    if pair_uniq(&mut p, pr.0, e2.b) { grew = true; }
                }
                j = j + 1i64;
            }
            k = k + 1i64;
        }
    }
    return p;
}

// ── ORACLE HALF 2: THE PRODUCT AUTOMATON. A SHAPE NEITHER ENGINE HAS.
// Walk the graph while carrying one bit — "have I used a deleted edge yet".
// States are (node, flag). From (n,0) an edge n→m goes to (m, 1) if it is
// deleted and (m, 0) otherwise; from (n,1) every edge stays in flag 1.
//     OD = { (x,z) : (z,1) reachable from (x,0) by a walk of ≥1 edge }
// which is literally "some derivation of (x,z) uses a deleted edge". This is
// NOT the last-deleted-edge decomposition used as the oracle in
// wql_incr_rel_od_phase1.logos — the two files' oracles can therefore not share
// a wrong theorem, and neither of them propagates a rel delta the way DRed does.
// Reachability is computed by naive relaxation over the encoded state set.
fn st_has(v: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, n: i64, f: i64) -> bool { return pair_has(v, n, f); }

fn automaton_od(all: &Vec<E>, del: &Vec<E>, nodes: &Vec<i64>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut od: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut xi: i64 = 0i64;
    while xi < nodes.len() {
        let x: i64 = nodes.get(xi);
        // reach = set of states (node, flag) reachable from (x,0) by ≥1 edge.
        let mut reach: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
        let mut grew: bool = true;
        // seed: one step out of (x,0).
        let mut e0: i64 = 0i64;
        while e0 < all.len() {
            let e: E = all.get(e0);
            if e.a == x {
                let f: i64 = if edge_in(del, e.a, e.b) { 1i64 } else { 0i64 };
                pair_uniq(&mut reach, e.b, f);
            }
            e0 = e0 + 1i64;
        }
        while grew {
            grew = false;
            let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
            while k < reach.len() {
                let s: (i64, i64) = reach.get(k);
                let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
                while j < all.len() {
                    let e2: E = all.get(j);
                    if e2.a == s.0 {
                        let d: i64 = if edge_in(del, e2.a, e2.b) { 1i64 } else { 0i64 };
                        let nf: i64 = if s.1 == 1i64 { 1i64 } else { d };
                        if pair_uniq(&mut reach, e2.b, nf) { grew = true; }
                    }
                    j = j + 1i64;
                }
                k = k + 1i64;
            }
        }
        let mut zi: i64 = 0i64;
        while zi < nodes.len() {
            let z: i64 = nodes.get(zi);
            if st_has(&reach, z, 1i64) { pair_uniq(&mut od, x, z); }
            zi = zi + 1i64;
        }
        xi = xi + 1i64;
    }
    return od;
}

fn edge_in(v: &Vec<E>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < v.len() {
        let e: E = v.get(i);
        if e.a == a && e.b == b { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

fn survivors(all: &Vec<E>, de: &Vec<E>) -> Vec<E> {
    let mut out: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < all.len() {
        let e: E = all.get(i);
        if !edge_in(de, e.a, e.b) { out.push(e); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// S = T ∖ OD, computed from the two independent oracles — never from the engine.
fn minus_set(t: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, od: &Vec<(i64, i64)>) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut out: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < t.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = t.get(i);
        if !pair_has(od, c.0, c.1) { pair_uniq(&mut out, c.0, c.1); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// ── engine plumbing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
fn mk_edge(d: &Writ, s: i64, t: i64) -> WAny {
    let e: Edge = d.make::<Edge>();
    e.src = s; e.dst = t;
    return WAny::ref_to((e.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn build_src(d: &Writ, hs: &Vec<WAny>, wts: &Vec<i64>) -> WAny {
    let arr: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(64i64);
    let n: i64 = hs.len();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < n {
        if wts.get(i) > 0i64 { arr.push(hs.get(i)); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return WAny::ref_to(arr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8);
}

fn snap_pairs(eng: &IncrRec) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let rs: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    let mut out: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut r: i64 = 0i64;
    while r < rs.row_count() {
        pair_uniq(&mut out, rs.get_i64(r, 0i64), rs.get_i64(r, 1i64));
        r = r + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

fn od_pairs(eng: &IncrRec) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut out: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let n: i64 = eng.od_len();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < n {
        let f: FactAB = eng.od_ab(i);
        pair_uniq(&mut out, f.a, f.b);
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// ── ONE CELL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Build the closure over ALL, retract DE in one epoch, then assert PHASE BY
// PHASE against the closed forms. `want_*` are the HAND-COMPUTED constants
// (witness 1) — they are checked against the ORACLES too, so an oracle and an
// engine agreeing on a wrong answer is still caught.
//
// Returns 0, or a code naming which check spoke.
fn cell(all: &Vec<E>, de: &Vec<E>, nodes: &Vec<i64>,
        want_t: i64, want_od: i64, want_s: i64, want_rd: i64, want_fin: i64) -> i32 {
    let pres: Vec<E> = survivors(all, de);

    // ── the oracles, all built before the engine is even asked ──
    let t_or:   Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(all);
    let od_or:  Vec<(i64, i64)> = automaton_od(all, de, nodes);
    let fin_or: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_closure(&pres);
    let s_or:   Vec<(i64, i64)> = minus_set(&t_or, &od_or);
    // RD = closure(PRES) ∖ S — the facts phase 1 threw away that are still true.
    let rd_or:  Vec<(i64, i64)> = minus_set(&fin_or, &s_or);
    // Witness 1 against witness 2: the hand constants pin every oracle.
    if t_or.len()   != want_t   { return 1i32; }
    if od_or.len()  != want_od  { return 2i32; }
    if s_or.len()   != want_s   { return 3i32; }
    if rd_or.len()  != want_rd  { return 4i32; }
    if fin_or.len() != want_fin { return 5i32; }
    // The decomposition identity, asserted rather than assumed:
    // closure(PRES) = S ⊎ RD, and S ⊆ closure(PRES) (a survivor derivation
    // stays valid). If this fails the oracles are inconsistent with each other.
    if want_s + want_rd != want_fin { return 6i32; }
    let mut si: i64 = 0i64;
    while si < s_or.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = s_or.get(si);
        if !pair_has(&fin_or, c.0, c.1) { return 7i32; }
        si = si + 1i64;
    }

    // ── the engine ──
    let doc: Writ = writ_new(65536i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();
    let q: Query = match Query::compile(
        r#"rel path(a: i64, b: i64) {
             from edges e select (e.src, e.dst);
             from path p join edges e on p.b == e.src select (p.a, e.dst);
           }
           from path p select (p.a, p.b)"#,
        &c)
    { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 10i32; } };

    let mut hs: Vec<WAny> = vec_new::<WAny>();
    let mut wts: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < all.len() {
        let e: E = all.get(i);
        hs.push(mk_edge(&doc, e.a, e.b));
        wts.push(1i64);
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    let mut env: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    env.bind_source("edges", build_src(&doc, &hs, &wts));
    let mut eng: IncrRec = match q.incremental_rec(&env)
        { Result::Ok(e2) => { e2 } Result::Err(_e3) => { return 11i32; } };

    // T — the accumulated total the retraction will act on.
    let t_eng: Vec<(i64, i64)> = snap_pairs(&eng);
    if !same_set(&t_eng, &t_or) { return 12i32; }
    // No pass has run: the latches must say so rather than report a stale set.
    if eng.dred_compact_len()  != 0i64 - 1i64 { return 13i32; }
    if eng.dred_rederive_len() != 0i64 - 1i64 { return 14i32; }
    if eng.od_len() != 0i64 { return 15i32; }

    // ── ONE EPOCH: retract every edge of DE ──
    let mut b: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    let mut di: i64 = 0i64;
    while di < de.len() {
        let d: E = de.get(di);
        let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
        while k < all.len() {
            let e3: E = all.get(k);
            if e3.a == d.a && e3.b == d.b { wts.set(k, 0i64); b.push(hs.get(k), 0i64 - 1i64); }
            k = k + 1i64;
        }
        di = di + 1i64;
    }
    eng.epoch(&b);
    if eng.had_error() { return 16i32; }

    // ── PHASE 1: the over-delete set, as an EXACT SET, against the automaton ──
    let od_eng: Vec<(i64, i64)> = od_pairs(&eng);
    if od_eng.len() != want_od { return 20i32; }
    if !same_set(&od_eng, &od_or) { return 21i32; }
    // OD ⊆ T — over-delete may never invent a fact the relation did not hold.
    let mut oi: i64 = 0i64;
    while oi < od_eng.len() {
        let c2: (i64, i64) = od_eng.get(oi);
        if !pair_has(&t_or, c2.0, c2.1) { return 22i32; }
        oi = oi + 1i64;
    }

    // ── PHASE 2: what COMPACT left. |S| = |T| − |OD| is an arithmetic identity
    // only if OD ⊆ T and neither side holds duplicates — both asserted above —
    // so this checks the compact filter itself, not the subtraction.
    if eng.dred_compact_len() != want_s { return 30i32; }
    if eng.dred_compact_len() != t_or.len() - od_eng.len() { return 31i32; }

    // ── PHASE 3: what RE-DERIVE put back. THE CELL AN ANSWER ORACLE CANNOT BE:
    // |RD| is invisible in `snapshot()` — the final answer is S ⊎ RD however the
    // work was split between phases 1 and 3.
    if eng.dred_rederive_len() != want_rd { return 40i32; }
    if eng.dred_compact_len() + eng.dred_rederive_len() != want_fin { return 41i32; }

    // ── THE ANSWER, still asserted, as an exact set over the survivors ──
    let fin_eng: Vec<(i64, i64)> = snap_pairs(&eng);
    if !same_set(&fin_eng, &fin_or) { return 50i32; }
    // …and every re-derived fact is one phase 1 had thrown away: RD ⊆ OD.
    let mut ri: i64 = 0i64;
    while ri < rd_or.len() {
        let c3: (i64, i64) = rd_or.get(ri);
        if !pair_has(&od_eng, c3.0, c3.1) { return 51i32; }
        if !pair_has(&fin_eng, c3.0, c3.1) { return 52i32; }
        ri = ri + 1i64;
    }
    return 0i32;
}

fn nodes4(a: i64, b: i64, c: i64, d: i64) -> Vec<i64> {
    let mut v: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    v.push(a); v.push(b); v.push(c); v.push(d);
    return v;
}

// ⚠ EXIT-CODE RANGES ARE DISJOINT ON PURPOSE. Cell codes reach 52 and the exit
// status is 8-bit, so the per-cell offsets are 60 / 130 / 190 (ranges 61..112,
// 131..182, 191..242) and the region-3 blocker cell uses 250/251. Overlapping
// ranges would make "which cell spoke" unreadable — the state-from-a-code-range
// gate lie.
fn main() -> i32 {
    // ══ CELL 1 — MID-CHAIN RETRACTION. Facts derived THROUGH the edge. ═════
    // ALL = 1→2, 2→3, 3→4;  DE = 2→3;  PRES = 1→2, 3→4.
    // T   = {12,23,34,13,24,14}                                  6
    // OD  = {23,24,13,14}   (every walk crossing 2→3)            4
    // S   = T ∖ OD = {12,34}                                     2
    // closure(PRES) = {12,34}                                    2
    // RD  = ∅                                                    0
    // ⚠ RD = 0 HERE BY DESIGN: this is the cell where COMPACT ALONE is already
    // the answer, so it isolates phase 2. (1,4) is derived at DEPTH 2 through
    // the deleted edge and never FROM it — a phase 1 that seeds only from the
    // base bodies leaves (1,3),(1,4) standing and |S| comes out 4.
    let mut a1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    a1.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); a1.push(ed(2i64, 3i64)); a1.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    let mut d1: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d1.push(ed(2i64, 3i64));
    let r1: i32 = cell(&a1, &d1, &nodes4(1i64, 2i64, 3i64, 4i64),
                       6i64, 4i64, 2i64, 0i64, 2i64);
    if r1 != 0i32 { return 60i32 + r1; }   // 61..112

    // ══ CELL 2 — THE DIAMOND. A FACT WITH TWO DERIVATIONS, ONE CUT. ════════
    // ALL = 1→2, 1→3, 2→4, 3→4;  DE = 2→4;  PRES = 1→2, 1→3, 3→4.
    // T   = {12,13,24,34,14}                                     5
    //       (1,4) has TWO derivations: 1→2→4 and 1→3→4.
    // OD  = {24,14}   — (1,4) IS over-deleted: one of its walks uses 2→4.
    // S   = {12,13,34}                                           3
    // closure(PRES) = {12,13,34,14}                              4
    // RD  = {14}                                                 1   ← PHASE 3
    // ⚠ THIS IS THE CELL PHASE 3 EXISTS FOR, and the reason an answer oracle is
    // blind: (1,4) is deleted by phase 1 and restored by phase 3, and the final
    // answer is the same whether that happened or phase 1 had spared it.
    // |RD| = 1 is the only assertion in this file that can tell those apart.
    let mut a2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    a2.push(ed(1i64, 2i64)); a2.push(ed(1i64, 3i64));
    a2.push(ed(2i64, 4i64)); a2.push(ed(3i64, 4i64));
    let mut d2: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d2.push(ed(2i64, 4i64));
    let r2: i32 = cell(&a2, &d2, &nodes4(1i64, 2i64, 3i64, 4i64),
                       5i64, 2i64, 3i64, 1i64, 4i64);
    if r2 != 0i32 { return 130i32 + r2; }  // 131..182

    // ══ CELL 3 — THE SELF-SUPPORTING CYCLE. WHAT COUNTING CANNOT DO. ═══════
    // ALL = 0→1, 1→2, 2→3, 3→1;  DE = 1→2 (a CYCLE edge);
    // PRES = 0→1, 2→3, 3→1.
    // T   = {(0,1),(0,2),(0,3)} ∪ {1,2,3}×{1,2,3}               12
    //       including (1,1),(2,2),(3,3): each cycle member supports itself
    //       THROUGH the cycle — under any scalar weight/count scheme those keep
    //       a positive count forever and NOTHING is retracted.
    // OD  = ALL OF T                                            12
    //       (every one of those walks can be routed across 1→2)
    // S   = ∅                                                    0   ← phase 2
    //       wipes the relation …
    // closure(PRES) = {(0,1),(2,3),(2,1),(3,1)}                  4
    // RD  = 4                                                    4   ← … and
    //       phase 3 rebuilds the entire answer. |S| = 0 with a CORRECT final
    //       answer is the sharpest statement that phase 2 alone is not an answer.
    let mut a3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    a3.push(ed(0i64, 1i64)); a3.push(ed(1i64, 2i64));
    a3.push(ed(2i64, 3i64)); a3.push(ed(3i64, 1i64));
    let mut d3: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    d3.push(ed(1i64, 2i64));
    let r3: i32 = cell(&a3, &d3, &nodes4(0i64, 1i64, 2i64, 3i64),
                       12i64, 12i64, 0i64, 4i64, 4i64);
    if r3 != 0i32 { return 190i32 + r3; }  // 191..242

    // ══ CELL 4 — THE BLOCKER FOR REGION 3, ASSERTED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. ═══
    // A 2-member mutual-recursion SCC compiles fine (the batch runner takes up
    // to 8 rel blocks) but `Query::incremental_rec` REFUSES it, so `dred` never
    // runs for ANY N-member SCC. That is why this file harvests nothing about
    // generic-N retraction: there is nothing at runtime to harvest.
    // ⚠ WHEN THIS CELL REDS, the refusal is gone and the region became
    // harvestable — write the N≥2 cells then, and delete this one.
    let c4: SchemaCatalog = cat();
    let q4: Query = match Query::compile(
        r#"rel ping(a: i64, b: i64) {
             from edges e select (e.src, e.dst);
             from pong p join edges e on p.b == e.src select (p.a, e.dst);
           }
           rel pong(a: i64, b: i64) {
             from ping p join edges e on p.b == e.src select (p.a, e.dst);
           }
           from ping p select (p.a, p.b)"#,
        &c4)
    { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 250i32; } };
    let doc4: Writ = writ_new(4096i64);
    let mut hs4: Vec<WAny> = vec_new::<WAny>();
    hs4.push(mk_edge(&doc4, 1i64, 2i64));
    hs4.push(mk_edge(&doc4, 2i64, 3i64));
    let mut w4: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    w4.push(1i64); w4.push(1i64);
    let mut env4: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    env4.bind_source("edges", build_src(&doc4, &hs4, &w4));
    match q4.incremental_rec(&env4) {
        Result::Ok(_e5) => { return 251i32; }   // ⇐ the refusal is gone: see above
        Result::Err(_e6) => { }
    }

    return 0i32;
}
